<tr><td>Standing-seam metal</td><td>Dented panels, damaged trim and flashing, loosened fasteners</td><td>Cosmetic damage exclusions</td></tr>
<tr><td>Tile (clay or concrete)</td><td>Cracked or broken tiles, damaged underlayment</td><td>Availability and matching of replacement tiles</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flat / low-slope commercial (TPO, modified bitumen, built-up)</td><td>Punctures, split seams, wet insulation</td><td>Moisture surveys; whether the membrane can be patched</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Composition shingles are the most common roof in the area. Standing-seam metal becomes more common toward the Hill Country edge in places like <a href="/service-areas/boerne/">Boerne</a> and <a href="/service-areas/helotes/">Helotes</a>. Older homes in historic districts such as King William, Monte Vista, and Tobin Hill may have older roof decks and multiple layers that complicate a tear-off.</p>

<h2>What Causes Covered Roof Damage</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hail.</strong> NOAA recorded hail up to 4.5 inches across northwest San Antonio on April 12, 2016 (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=623870" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event record</a>), one of the costliest hailstorms in Texas history.</li>
<li><strong>Wind.</strong> On February 19, 2017, thunderstorm winds estimated at 70 mph tore part of the roof off a house near the Olmos Basin in northern San Antonio (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=674892" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI</a>). See <a href="/services/wind-damage-claims/">wind damage claims</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Falling trees and limbs</strong> during storms or ice events.</li>
<li><strong>Ice accumulation</strong> on gutters and limbs during freezing rain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Roof leaks caused by age, deterioration, poor installation, or lack of maintenance are generally excluded. When the insurer and the owner disagree about cause, the evidence on the roof matters most.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Hail-Damage-Roof-Insurance-Claim-How-to-Protect-Your-Roof-5-1024x683.webp" alt="Roofer inspecting damaged shingles on a residential roof while recording notes on a tablet" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A slope-by-slope inspection records the type and extent of damage before any repairs are made.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How Insurers Value a Roof Claim</h2>
<h3>Repair vs. replacement</h3>
<p>Insurers often start with a repair estimate. Whether a full replacement is warranted can depend on how many slopes are damaged, whether brittle shingles can be lifted and replaced without breaking neighbors, and whether matching materials are available.</p>
<h3>Actual cash value and depreciation</h3>
<p>Under a replacement cost policy, the insurer typically pays actual cash value first (replacement cost minus depreciation) and releases recoverable depreciation after repairs are complete, often within a deadline in the policy. Some Texas policies cover roofs on an ACV-only basis or use a roof payment schedule based on age. Read more in <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a>.</p>
<h3>Deductibles</h3>
<p>Wind and hail roof claims often fall under a separate percentage deductible based on the dwelling limit. Bexar County is not in the TWIA area, so these deductibles come from your regular homeowners or commercial policy. Check your declarations page.</p>
<h3>Code and permit items</h3>
<p>Re-roofing inside city limits generally requires a permit from the City of San Antonio Development Services Department, and the work must meet the city's adopted International Residential Code and International Building Code. Code-required items are paid only to the extent your policy includes ordinance or law coverage. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a>.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>Line Items Often Missing From Roof Estimates</h2>
<p>When we review a roof estimate, these items come up repeatedly:</p>
<ul>
<li>Starter course, drip edge, and ridge cap</li>
<li>Step flashing, counter flashing, and pipe jacks</li>
<li>Underlayment and ice-and-water barrier where required</li>
<li>Additional layers to tear off and decking replacement</li>
<li>Steep and high-roof charges, and permit costs</li>
<li>Gutters, downspouts, and roof vents damaged in the same storm</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> compares the insurer's scope against the actual roof. If a claim was paid but left items out, an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> may be appropriate.</p>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster Helps With Roof Claims</h2>
<p>San Antonio Public Adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurer. On a roof claim, that may include inspecting each slope, photographing and measuring damage, reviewing your policy and endorsements, preparing a line-item estimate in Xactimate, and meeting the insurer's adjuster on the roof. If the disagreement is about the amount of loss rather than coverage, we can explain whether the <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> clause may apply.</p>
<p>A public adjuster does not repair roofs. Texas conflict-of-interest rules under Insurance Code Chapter 4102 bar a public adjuster from taking part in repairs on a claim they adjust, so your roofer remains your choice. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. No one can guarantee a particular result.</p>

<h2>When the Insurer Says the Damage Is Old</h2>
<p>A frequent roof denial says the damage is from wear and tear, or from a storm before the policy started. San Antonio's summer heat does age shingles, and some neighborhoods see hail more than once in a season, so the insurer's argument is not always unreasonable. The answer is evidence: dated photos of the roof before and after the storm, NOAA reports near the address, fresh exposed mat at impact points, and matching marks on gutters, vents, and AC fins. If you never photographed the roof before the storm, older listing photos, satellite imagery, or a prior inspection report can sometimes fill the gap.</p>

<h2>Commercial and Multi-Family Roofs</h2>
<p>Flat and low-slope roofs on retail centers, offices, warehouses, and apartment buildings are harder to evaluate because damage to the membrane may not be visible. Wet insulation under an intact-looking surface can spread and fail later. Core samples and moisture surveys are often part of documenting these claims, and the policy's business income coverage may come into play if the building cannot be used.</p>

<h2>Steps to Take After Roof Damage</h2>
<ol>
<li>Photograph the roof, gutters, and any interior stains from several angles.</li>
<li>Tarp any opening that lets water in and save the receipt.</li>
<li>Report the claim promptly and write down the claim number.</li>
<li>Pull your declarations page and look for roof schedules or cosmetic exclusions.</li>
<li>Be present for the inspection, or have your representative there.</li>
<li>Get the insurer's estimate in writing and review it line by line.</li>
</ol>
